course: dictionary
lesson: 3223
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Enterprise» can mean a business, a bold plan, or get-up-and-go, the willingness to try new things. Context tells you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: In «the town of Enterprise built a statue to it», Enterprise is the name of a place, with a capital letter. Swap in a meaning like business and see if it fits. It does not, so it is a name here.
explain: «She showed great enterprise» is praise. It means she was keen to try new things and work hard. Ask yourself which meaning fits the words around it.
fact: Enterprise means a business or company, such as a shop or a firm.
fact: A big, new or bold plan or project can also be called an enterprise.
fact: Enterprise can mean get-up-and-go: being willing to try new things and work hard to make them happen.
fact: A capital letter, as in «the town of Enterprise», shows a name of a place, not a meaning of the word.
exercise: «In Alabama, the town of Enterprise built a statue to the boll weevil.» What is Enterprise here? | A bold plan | A business | *The name of a town | Get-up-and-go
exercise: «She showed great enterprise.» What does enterprise mean? | *Get-up-and-go, being willing to try and work hard | A grey, wet day | A statue | A small shop
exercise: «Small enterprises are important in every town.» What does enterprises mean? | Tiny insects | *Small businesses | Small holes in the ground | Old castles
test: «Free enterprise means people can run businesses freely.» What does free enterprise mean? | Being given free food | Free tickets to a show | *People can run businesses freely | A free school
test: Mr Shah's class planned a school enterprise to sell cakes at lunch. What is the best meaning of enterprise here? | A grey, wet day | *A business or a bold plan | A sort of whisper | A large animal
exam: Which sentence uses enterprise to mean a bold plan? | Dad drank a cup of tea | *Our class launched an enterprise to plant trees in the park | The boll weevil ate the cotton in Alabama | The castle stood on the hill
